## ABSTRACT
To develop high-performance metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) for catalysis is of great importance. Here, we synthesized the mesoporous $\mathsf{C u}_{3-x}Z\mathsf{n}_{x}(\mathsf{B T C})_{2}$ (BTC $\mathbf{\tau}=\mathbf{\tau}$ benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxylate) nanocubes in a deep eutectic solvent of $Z_{n}C l_{2}$ /ethylene glycol solution. The route can proceed at room temperature and the reaction time needed is shortened to be $30~\mathrm{min}$ , which is superior to the conventional solvothermal route that usually needs high temperature and long reaction time. The formation mechanism of the  mesoporous $\mathsf{C u}_{3-x}Z\mathsf{n}_{x}(\mathsf{B T C})_{2}$ nanocubes  in  deep  eutectic  solvent  (DES)  was  investigated  by in  situ  synchrotron $\mathsf{x}$ -ray diffraction/small angle $\mathsf{X}$ -ray scattering/X-ray absorption fine structure conjunction technique. The mesoporous $\mathsf{C u}_{3-x}Z\mathsf{n}_{x}(\mathsf{B T C})_{2}$ nanocubes  exhibit  high  catalytic  activity  and  reusability  for  cyanosilylation  reaction  of  benzaldehyde  and  aerobic  oxidation reaction of benzylic alcohol.
## KEYWORDS
bimetallic  metal-organic  framework  (MOF),  mesoporous  nanocubes,  deep  eutectic  solvent,  formation  mechanism, catalytic performance
## Introduction
Metal-organic  frameworks  (MOFs)  are  an  appealing  class  of crystalline  porous  materials  composed  of  metal  cations/clusters and organic linkers [1], which have been widely used in fields of gas  storage  and  separation  [2–4],  catalysis  [5, 6],  sensor  [7], theragnosis  [8, 9],  etc.  Particularly,  MOFs  exhibit  catalytic potentials  towards  many  reactions  owing  to  their  rich compositions  and  modifiable  structures  [10].  The  catalytic performances of MOFs are mainly restricted by (1) relatively low activity  resulting  from  the  obstruction  of  active  metal  sites  by organic linkers and resistance of mass transfer [11] and (2) poor stability.  Diverse  kinds  of  methods  have  been  proposed  to overcome these limitations. One of the mostly adopted route is by introducing a secondary catalytic phase into MOF, but suffering from synthesis complexity and phase separation during catalysis process.  Another  useful  route  is  through  nanoengineering  for MOFs,  e.g.,  fabricating  low-dimensional  nanostructures  [6], constructing  hierachically  porous  structures  [12],  and  designing defects [13]. Compared with MOF-based composite catalyst, the use  of  single  MOF  as  catalyst  is  more  attractive  for  the  ease  in preparation,  avoidance  of  phase  separation,  and  well-controlled performance through structure tailoring, but is challenging.
Solvothermal  route  is  the  common  method  for  synthesizing MOFs.  The  usually  used  solvents  are  organic  liquids  (e.g., N,Ndimethylformamide  (DMF)  and  dimethyl  sulfoxide)  or  their mixed  solvent  with  ethanol  or  water.  In  most  cases,  high temperature $\mathrm{(>100~^{\circ}C)}$ and long reaction time (couples of days) are required to overcome the high enthalpies of MOF formation or  account  for  the  slow  kinetics  of  nucleation  [14–16].  Deep eutectic  solvent  (DES)  is  an  extended  class  of  ionic  liquid constructed  from  H-bond  donor  and  acceptor  via  hydrogen bonding [17]. For the easy availability from relatively inexpensive components,  biodegradability,  and  nontoxicity  [18],  DESs  have been  utilized  in  various  areas  such  as  metallurgy  [19], biotransformation  [20, 21],  and  nanomaterial  synthesis  [22–24]. There  are  a  few  reports  on  using  DES  as  an  alternative  of conventional  solvent  for  MOF  synthesis.  For  example,  choline chloride/glycerol DES was used for synthesizing UiO-66 at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ [25];  choline  chloride/urea  DES  was  utilized  for producing Cu-BDC at $45^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $6\mathrm{{h}}$ [26] and HKUST-1 at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for  3  days,  [27],  respectively.  It  is  interesting  to  develop  MOF catalysts with delicate structures and high performances by fully utilizing the advantages of DES.
Herein,  we  propose  for  the  first  time  the  utilization  of  zinc chloride $(\mathrm{ZnCl}_{2})$ /ethylene  glycol  (EG)  DES  for  the  formation  of $\mathrm{{(Cu,Zn)}}$ -bimetallic  MOF.  Interestingly,  the  route  can  proceed  at room  temperature  and  the  reaction  time  needed  is  shortened extremely  to  be $30~\mathrm{\min}$ .  By  the  strategy,  the  mesoporous nanocubes  of $\mathrm{{(cu,Zn)}}$ -bimetallic  MOF  were  obtained.  The formation mechanism for such a novel MOF structure in DES was researched by in situ synchrotron X-ray diffraction/small angle Xray scattering/X-ray absorption fine structure (XRD/SAXS/XAFS) conjunction  technique.  Owing  to  the  small  particle  size, mesoporous  structure,  and  bimetallic  feature,  the $\mathrm{{(Cu,Zn)}}$ - bimetallic  MOF  exhibits  high  activity  and  stability  for  the cyanosilylation  reaction  of  benzaldehyde  and  aerobic  oxidation reaction of benzylic alcohol.
## 2    Results and discussion
To get the target product, the desired amount of copper $\left(\mathrm{II}\right)$ acetate monohydrate and 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid was added into the pre-prepared $\mathrm{ZnCl}_{2}/\mathrm{EG}$ DES (with $\mathrm{ZnCl}_{2}$ to EG molar ratio of 1:6).  After  stirring  at  room  temperature  for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ ,  the  solid product was obtained by washing and drying.
For  comparison, $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ was  synthesized  by  a  common solvothermal route in DMF/ethanol/water at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10\mathrm{{h}}$ [32]. The product presents as solid particles in micron (Fig. S3 in the ESM).  Its $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption  isotherm  exhibits  type  I mode  corresponding  to  microporous  MOF  (Fig. 1(e)).  The  BET surface  area  and  pore  volume  are $936.7~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $0.56~\mathrm{cm^{3}{\cdot}g^{-1}};$ respectively  (Table  S2  in  the  ESM).  The  decreased  BET  surface area of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ than $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ can be attributed to the interpenetration  of  the  pores  [33].  The  micropore  volume  of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ $(0.19~\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ is lower than that of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2},$ suggesting  lowered  crystallinity,  which  is  consistent  with  XRD results.
The  insightful  chemical  and  structural  information  of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ was  investigated  by  X-ray  photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, Fig. S4 in the ESM). For Cu 2p XPS spectrum, the  Cu $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ region  shows  two  distinct  peaks  at  935.0  and $932.9~\mathrm{eV}$ , which are assigned to $\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}$ and $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ , respectively (Fig. 2(a)). The Cu LMM Auger spectrum further proves the presence of ${{\mathrm{Cu}}^{+}}$ in $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ (Fig. $2(\mathrm{b})_{\cdot}^{\cdot}$ ), which can be ascribed to the  linker  missing  in  framework  structure  along  with  the formation of mesoporous $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ [6]. For $\mathtt{Z n2p}$ XPS spectrum, there is a single Zn $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ peak at $1{,}022{.}4\ \mathrm{eV};$ , implying that $Z\mathrm{n}$ exists as $+2$ valence state in $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ (Fig. 2(c)) [28, 34].  The  O  1s  XPS  spectrum  of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ presents two  curve-fitted  peaks  at  531.9  and $533.5~\mathrm{eV}$ corresponding  to $\mathrm{Cu/Zn{\mathrm{-}}O}$ and  C–O  species,  respectively  (Fig. $2(\mathrm{d})_{,}^{\cdot}$ .  The  relative strength  of $\mathrm{{Cu/Zn{-}O}}$ to $C{\mathrm{-}}0$ in $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ is  obviously higher  than  that  of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2},$ indicating  that  more $\mathrm{Cu/Zn–O}$ clusters in $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ nanocubes [6].
To  further  detect  the  local  structure  of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ at atomic  level,  XAFS  was  determined.  For  Cu  K-edge $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray absorption  near-edge  structure  (XANES), $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ exhibits  similar  spectrum  features  to  those  of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ (Fig. 2(e)),  implying  that  their  main  structures  are  the  same.  The absorption  edge  of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ is  at $8{,}989.6~\mathrm{eV},$ which  is between those of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ $(8{,}989{.}8\mathrm{eV})$ and Cu foil $(8,979.0\mathrm{eV})$ . It  indicates  the  existence  of $\mathrm{Cu}^{\delta+}$ $(0~<~\delta~<~2)$ )  species  in $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ . From extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS)  spectra  (Fig. $2(\mathrm{f}))$ ,  the  peak  centered  at $1.45\mathrm{~\AA~}$ for $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ shows an intensity drop, which further reveals lower coordination number of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ nanocubes [35]. In  Zn  K-edge  XANES  (Fig. $2(\mathrm{g)}^{\cdot}$ ),  the  pre-edge  position  of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ is located closer to that of $\mathrm{znO}_{\mathrm{:}}$ , suggesting that Zn  exists  in  the  form  of $+2$ valence  state  in $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ . Meanwhile,  the  EXAFS  spectrum  shows  a  dominant  peak  at $1.53\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ (Fig. $2(\mathrm{h})_{\cdot}^{\cdot}$ ), belonging to the coordination bond between $Z\mathrm{n}$ and O in $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ [36].
The formation of the micro- and mesoporous $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ nanocubes  is  very  interesting.  To  reveal  the formation mechanism, a series of experiments were carried out. First,  the in  situ synchrotron  XRD/SAXS/XAFS  conjunction technique  was  used  to  trace  the  synthesis  process  of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ in $Z\mathrm{nCl}_{2}/\mathrm{EG}$ DES.  The in  situ XRD  results reveal  the  formation  of  crystallined  framework  structure  at  the very beginning stage of $10\ \mathrm{min}.$ , and the structure is maintained with prolonged reaction time (Fig. 3(a)). Meanwhile, the relative intensities $(I)$ of SAXS curves shift to high scattering wavevector $(q)$ with  increasing  reaction  time  (Fig. 3(b)),  corresponding  to larger  particle  size  [37].  From  the  pair-distance  distribution function $p(r)$ curves (inset in Fig. $3(\mathrm{b})$ ), the sizes of particles in the reaction system at reaction time of 5, 10, 15, 20, and $30\mathrm{min}$ are 8, 25,  53,  85,  and $100\ \mathrm{nm}$ ,  respectively.  The in  situ SAXS  results reveal  that  the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ particles  grow  larger  with prolonged  reaction  time.  Over  time,  both  the  intensities  of  the white line (first resonance after the edge) in Cu K-edge XANES (Fig. 3(c))  and  the  signal  at $R=1.45\mathrm{~\r~{~\AA~}~}$ in  EXAFS  spectra  (Fig. $3(\mathrm{d})_{,}^{\cdot}$ ) increase, confirming the coordination of carboxylate groups to Cu ions. For Zn K-edge XANES, the white line intensity first increases in $10~\mathrm{min}$ and then decreases (Fig. 3(e)), as well as the peak centered at $1.53\mathrm{\AA}$ in EXAFS spectra (Fig. 3(f)). It implies the coordination of carboxylate groups to $Z\mathrm{n}$ ions in $10\mathrm{min}$ and then the gradual dissociation with prolonged reaction time.
Second,  the  evolution  process  of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ was investigated by varying the synthesis time from 5 to $30~\mathrm{min}$ , the other experimental conditions being the same with those above. The  products  obtained  at  different  reaction  times  were characterized  by  TEM.  At  reaction  time  of $10\ \mathrm{min}.$ ,  small  MOF cubic nanoparticles come into being (Fig. S5 in the ESM). Then more  and  more  pores  emerge  inside  the  nanocubes  with prolonged reaction time. The $\mathrm{{Cu/Zn}}$ molar ratios of the products synthesized at 10, 15, and $20~\mathrm{min}$ were determined to be 1.9, 3.0, and 4.5, respectively. Simultaneously, the amounts of mesopores increase with processing time, as evidenced by $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsroption–desorption  isotherms  (Fig. S6  and  Table  S3  in  the ESM). These results suggest that the hollowness of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ is related with the dissociation of $Z\mathrm{n}$ ions from the framework. By combination of the above results with in situ XRD/SAXS/XAFE  conjunction  technique,  it  can  be  concluded that  the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ goes  through  a  rapid  crystallization process  and  then  an  etching  process  for  mesoporous  structure formation.
Third,  a  control  experiment  using  pure  EG  (instead  of $\mathrm{ZnCl}_{2}/\mathrm{EG}$ DES)  as  solvent  for $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ was  conducted.  The reaction time was $30~\mathrm{min}$ and the other experimental conditions were  the  same  with  those  for $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ synthesis.  XRD pattern reveals the formation of crystalline $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ (Fig. S7 in the ESM). Different from the morphology of $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{2.55}\mathrm{{Zn}}_{0.45}(\mathrm{{BTC})_{2},}$ the product presents particle aggregates without mesopores (Fig. S7 in the ESM). The results prove that $\mathrm{ZnCl}_{2}$ is dominant for the formation  of  mesoporous  structure  of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ nanocubes. Based on all the above results, a possible mechanism for  the  formation  of  the  mesoporous $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ nanocubes in $\mathrm{ZnCl}_{2}/\mathrm{EG}$ DES is proposed. In the initial stage, Cu ions and $Z\mathrm{n}$ ions co-coordinate with the deprotonated $\mathrm{{H}}_{3}\mathrm{{BTC}}$ to construct nanosized framework. Since the coordination of $Z\mathrm{n}$ ion with  deprotonated $\mathrm{{H}}_{3}\mathrm{{BTC}}$ is  weaker  than  that  of $\mathrm{Cu}$ ion,  the framework  retains  the $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ crystalline  lattice.  Then  the weakly  bonded  linker  to $Z\mathrm{n}$ ions  can  be  dissociated  from framework, which could provide copious sites for further etching and afterwards enable the creation of mesopores in $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ nanocubes [38].
Considering that $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ has high pore volume and abundant coordinatively unsaturated metal nodes, it was used as a Lewis  acid  catalyst  for  the  cyanosilylation  of  aldehydes  to  the corresponding cyanohydrins, which is a class of important organic intermediates  for  both  biological  processes  and  synthetic chemistry  [39].  The  reaction  was  carried  out  at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under solvent-free  condition  [40].  The  product  was  determined  by $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR). For comparison, the catalytic performance of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ for the cyanosilylation of benzaldehyde was also studied at the same experimental conditions. Remarkably, there  is  a  significant  increase  in  benzaldehyde  conversion  as catalyzed  by $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ compared  with $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ (Fig. 4(a)).  At $8\ \mathrm{h}.$ benzaldehyde  can  be  converted  completely  to  2- phenyl-2-[(trimethylsilyl)oxy]  acetonitrile  by $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ .
withdrawing  group  attached  to  the  phenyl  ring  (–F,  conversion $97\%;$ ; $-\mathrm{NO}_{2},$ conversion $94\%$ ) are much more reactive than those with an electron-donating group $\left(\mathrm{-CH}_{3},\right.$ conversion $73\%$ ).
Moreover, $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ catalyst was used for the selective oxidation of benzyl alcohol. The results show that $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ catalysts are much more active and stable than $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ (Figs. S11–S14 and Table S5 in the ESM).
The high activity of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ can be mainly ascribed to  its  hierarchically  micro-mesoporous  and  nanosized  structure [41, 42].  Such  a  structure  results  in  the  existence  of  abundant unsaturated coordination $\mathrm{Cu}$ sites on surfaces, which could act as Lewis  acid  sites  to  bind  with  the  O  atoms  of  the  aldehydes  to activate  the  carbonyl  C  atoms  followed  by  reaction  with trimethylsilyl  cyanide  [39, 40].  Moreover,  the  mesoporous structure provides more accessible active sites, improves substrate adsorption, and facilitates mass diffusion during reaction [41]. The Zn  nodes  not  only  induce  the  mesopore  formation  of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ for improving catalyst activity, but play a role of enhancing  catalyst  stability  [43–46].  The $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ catalyst  keeps  its  crystal  structure  after  four  runs,  while  the framework of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ is destroyed after three catalytic runs. It indicates that the $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions are efficient in preserving the stability of  framework  during  catalytic  process,  similar  to  the  results reported in Ref. [43].
## 3 Conclusions
In  summary,  we  synthesized  mesoporous $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ nanocubes  in $Z\mathrm{nCl}_{2}/\mathrm{EG}$ DES.  This  strategy  produces  bimetallic MOF with small particle size $\cdot\sim100\ \mathrm{nm})$ , hierarchical micro- and mesopores,  and  abundant  unsaturated  coordination  metal  sites. The as-synthesized $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ nanocubes have shown high efficiency and reusability for the cyanosilylation of benzaldehyde and the aerobic oxidation of benzylic alcohol. The proposed DES route  for  MOF  synthesis  is  facile,  rapid,  and  environmentally benign and can proceed at room-temperature. We anticipate that it could be applied to the fabrication of other MOFs and MOFbased materials with desirable compositions and functions.
By contrast, the conversion of benzaldehyde is only $16\%$ catalyzed by $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ . It is worth noting that the catalytic performance of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ is  much  better  than  those  of  the  reported $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ under  similar  reaction  conditions  (Table  S4  in  the ESM).
After reaction for $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ , the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ was recovered and its  reusability  for  the  reaction  was  tested.  The  conversion  of benzaldehyde  remains  unchanged  after  the $4^{\mathrm{{th}}}$ run  for  catalysis (Fig. $4(\mathrm{b})_{\cdot}^{\cdot}$ ).  No  obvious  difference  was  observed  for  the  XRD patterns, TEM images, and XPS spectra of the fresh and reused $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ (Figs.  S8  and  S9  in  the  ESM),  indicating  the well  preserved  structural  integrity  of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ catalyst. ICP-AES  was  used  to  determine  whether  there  were  metal  ions leached to filtrate after reaction for four runs. No copper ions or zinc  ions  were  detected,  further  confirming  the  stability  of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2.55}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.45}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ during catalysis. By contrast, when $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ was used as catalyst, $30\%$ yield was lost for the third run. XRD pattern  of  the  reused $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ catalyst  reveals  that  the framework structure was destroyed (Fig. S10 in the ESM).
